## 4. Proposed Mechanisms for Maintaining Vitality

### 4.1 Periodic Self-Assessment

| Action | Frequency | Purpose |
|--------|-----------|---------|
| **Review the manifesto** | Every 5 years | Is it still relevant? Should it be revised? |
| **Review the conditions of belonging** | Every 5 years | Are they still appropriate? Have they become exclusionary? |
| **Assess diversity of practitioners** | Annually | Is the movement attracting new voices? |
| **Identify signs of petrification** | Annually | Are formulas replacing experimentation? |

### 4.2 Inviting External Critique
| Action | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **Open peer review** | Invite critics, scholars, and other artists to critique the movement |
| **Response mechanism** | Publish responses, even critical ones |
| **Annual "State of the Movement" report** | Transparency about the movement's health |

### 4.3 Open Calls for Contributions

| Action | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **Annual open call** | Invite artists to submit works for consideration |
| **No fee, no jury** | Reduce barriers to participation |
| **Transparent criteria** | Publish how works are evaluated |
| **Inclusion of rejected works in archive** | Document what was not accepted |

### 4.4 Anti-Canonical Practices
| Action | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **No "essential works" list** | Avoid designating a canon |
| **Rotating emphasis** | Different works are highlighted in different contexts |
| **Emphasis on principles, not exemplars** | The principles matter more than any particular work |
| **Celebrate diversity** | Show works that differ from the founder's |

### 4.5 Succession Planning
| Action | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **No single successor** | Avoid creating a new "founder" |
| **Rotating leadership** | If leadership is needed, rotate it |
| **Movement belongs to practitioners** | Emphasize that no one owns the movement |
| **Legal structure (if needed)** | A foundation or collective, not a sole proprietorship |

4.6 The "Open Horizon" Principle
The manifesto of Post-Post-Perception states:
> "Post-Post-Perception is an open horizon, an infinite movement, a perpetual laboratory for consciousness."
**This principle extends the openness of Post-Perception into the future.**
5. Learning from the Failures of Other Movements
5.1 What Surrealism Did Wrong
| Error | Lesson |
|-------|--------|
| Founder autocracy (Breton's expulsions) | No single person should have the power to exclude |
| Purity tests | Conditions of belonging should not become stricter over time |
| Canon fixation | Avoid designating "essential" works |
| Successor vacuum | Plan for continuity without a founder |
### 5.2 What De Stijl Did Wrong
| Error | Lesson |
|-------|--------|
| Dependence on a single organizer (Van Doesburg) | Distribute organizational responsibility |
| Rigid aesthetic formula | Avoid reducing the movement to a visual style |
| Ended with founder's death | Plan for continuity |
### 5.3 What Conceptual Art Did (Partially) Right
| Practice | Lesson |
|----------|--------|
| Multiple originators | Multiple founders reduce dependence on one person |
| Anti-institutional stance | Remains suspicious of institutional co-optation |
| Emphasis on idea over object | Resists commodification |

### 5.4 What Fluxus Did Right
| Practice | Lesson |
|----------|--------|
| Open membership | Anyone could participate |
| Anti-hierarchy | No central authority |
| Ephemerality | Resists canonization through impermanence |
| Playful, anti-dogmatic attitude | Refuses to take itself too seriously |
